The purpose of this phase is:

TRAIN → EVALUATE → BACKTEST → COMPARE AGAINST BASELINE.

Only if the ML model demonstrably beats the deterministic baseline should we consider integrating it into the live projection engine.

==================================================
1. DEFINE THE PREDICTION TASKS
==================================================

Build separate predictive tasks rather than one giant "minutes model".

We want to predict:

MODEL A:
P(start)

MODEL B:
Expected minutes

MODEL C:
P(60+ minutes)

MODEL D:
P(0 minutes)

Use the existing Phase 2A historical dataset.

Before implementation, inspect the actual dataset schema and verify the available features.

Do NOT invent features that aren't actually available.

==================================================
2. BASELINE MODELS
==================================================

Before training ML, establish explicit deterministic baselines.

For each target, define a reasonable baseline using only information available in the historical snapshot.

At minimum compare against:

P(start) baseline:
recent-start/minutes-based heuristic

Expected minutes baseline:
recent-minutes / starts-based heuristic

P(60+) baseline:
recent frequency of playing 60+ minutes

P(0) baseline:
recent frequency of 0-minute appearances

Use the existing deterministic minutes logic where appropriate.

Document the exact baseline formulas.

The ML models must beat these baselines OUT-OF-SAMPLE.

==================================================
