- price
- position
- team
- opponent
- home/away
- fixture difficulty
- team attack rating
- team defence rating
- opponent attack rating
- opponent defence rating
- days since last match
- fixture congestion
- relevant availability/status fields

Do NOT automatically use every available feature.

If a feature is suspicious, redundant, unavailable historically, or potentially leaky, flag it before training.

==================================================
4. TEMPORAL LEAKAGE REQUIREMENT
==================================================

This is non-negotiable.

For a prediction for GW N:

ONLY information available before the GW N deadline may be used.

The target outcome must never appear as a feature.

Do NOT randomly split rows.

Use the existing chronological split:

TRAIN:
2022/23 + 2023/24

VALIDATION:
2024/25

TEST:
2025/26

The 2025/26 TEST SET must remain untouched during model selection and hyperparameter tuning.

Do NOT use test-set performance to choose the model.

==================================================
5. MODEL A — P(START)
==================================================

Train a binary classification model predicting:

target_started

Candidate models:

- LightGBM
- XGBoost
- Logistic Regression baseline

Start with a simple, interpretable implementation.

Do not assume LightGBM/XGBoost automatically wins.

Evaluate:

- Log Loss
- Brier Score
- ROC-AUC
- PR-AUC
- Calibration

Calibration is particularly important.

A prediction of:

P(start) = 0.80

should actually correspond to roughly an 80% start rate over sufficiently large groups.

Produce a calibration analysis.

==================================================
6. MODEL B — EXPECTED MINUTES
==================================================

Train a regression model predicting:

target_minutes

Candidate models:

- LightGBM
- XGBoost
- Gradient Boosting
- simple baseline regression where useful

Evaluate:

- MAE
- RMSE
- Median Absolute Error

Also report performance separately for useful groups where sample size allows:

- starters
- substitutes
- defenders
- midfielders
- forwards
- goalkeepers

Be careful not to overfit tiny subgroups.

==================================================
7. MODEL C — P(60+)
==================================================

Train a classifier predicting:

target_60_plus

Evaluate:

- Log Loss
- Brier Score
- ROC-AUC
- PR-AUC
- Calibration

Compare against the deterministic frequency baseline.

==================================================
8. MODEL D — P(0)
==================================================

Train a classifier predicting:

target_zero_minutes

Evaluate:

- Log Loss
- Brier Score
- ROC-AUC
- PR-AUC
- Calibration

Pay particular attention to class imbalance.

Do NOT judge this model solely by accuracy.

A model that predicts "0 minutes" for nobody can still achieve misleadingly high accuracy if zero-minute outcomes are sufficiently uncommon.

==================================================
