10. OUT-OF-SAMPLE EVALUATION
==================================================

Create a clear comparison table.

Example:

MODEL | TARGET | BASELINE | ML | IMPROVEMENT

For example:

Baseline | P(start) | LogLoss X | — | —
LightGBM | P(start) | — | LogLoss Y | Z%

Do this for:

P(start)
Expected minutes
P(60+)
P(0)

Clearly identify whether ML actually beats the baseline.

==================================================
11. CALIBRATION
==================================================

For all probability models, produce calibration results.

Check whether:

Predicted probability
≈
Observed frequency

Do not optimize only for discrimination.

An FPL decision engine needs reliable probabilities.

For example:

If the model says:

P(start) = 0.90

we need that to mean something operationally.

==================================================
12. FEATURE IMPORTANCE / INTERPRETABILITY
==================================================

For the selected ML model, produce feature importance.

Prefer interpretable methods such as:

- LightGBM feature importance
- permutation importance
- SHAP if already supported and practical

Do NOT add unnecessary dependencies just for visualizations.

We want to understand what drives the model.

For example, it would be sensible if:

recent starts
recent minutes
recent substitution history

are highly influential.

If an obviously suspicious feature dominates the model, investigate it.

==================================================
13. ERROR ANALYSIS
==================================================

Do not stop at aggregate metrics.

Identify examples where the model performs poorly.

Inspect cases such as:

- nailed starter unexpectedly benched
- substitute unexpectedly started
- injury/rotation cases
- players returning from injury
- new signings
- fixture congestion
- players with very small samples

Show at least several representative errors.

The objective is to understand where the model fails.

==================================================
14. SMALL-SAMPLE BEHAVIOUR
==================================================

Pay particular attention to players with limited historical minutes.

The model must not overreact to tiny samples.

For example:

A player with 180 historical minutes should not automatically receive extreme confidence because of three matches.

Check model behaviour for:

- < 300 minutes
- 300–600 minutes
- 600–1000 minutes
- >1000 minutes

where sample sizes permit.

Report whether performance degrades for small samples.

